WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.039
Welcome back. Plenty of production work has no one sitting there waiting for it.

2
00:00:05.039 --> 00:00:09.359
Overnight reports, weekly audits, backfilling a corpus of documents.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.319
This lesson covers the Message Batches API, what batching trades away for its discount,

4
00:00:16.319 --> 00:00:20.559
and how to tell which of your workloads actually qualify. Let's dive in.

5
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.559
Start with a concrete picture. You have 10,000 documents to run extraction over.

6
00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:30.239
The prompt is the same for every one of them, the model is the same,

7
00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:35.119
and the output you want is the same. The only real decision is how you deliver the work.

8
00:00:35.759 --> 00:00:41.119
Send them synchronously, and you're waiting on each response before the next one goes out.

9
00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:47.119
Or, hand the whole set over as a batch, walk away, and collect the results when they're ready.

10
00:00:47.119 --> 00:00:51.840
Here's the part worth being clear about. You aren't accepting a weaker or cheaper model

11
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.880
in exchange for the discount. Same model, same prompt, same quality of output.

12
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.360
What you're trading is time. The Message Batches API is the

13
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.879
endpoint that takes many requests at once and processes them asynchronously,

14
00:01:06.879 --> 00:01:10.319
and it bills token usage at half the standard rate.

15
00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:13.360
The processing window is how long the batch is allowed to take.

16
00:01:13.919 --> 00:01:18.639
Most finish inside an hour, but the number that matters for your design is the ceiling,

17
00:01:18.639 --> 00:01:22.639
which is 24 hours, and there's no latency guarantee inside it.

18
00:01:22.639 --> 00:01:27.680
The custom identifier is a label you attach to each request when you submit it.

19
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.400
It looks like bookkeeping, and it is the single most important field in the whole workflow.

20
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.000
We'll come back to why shortly.

21
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.160
So, what are you actually giving up for that 50%?

22
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.480
Three things, and they're worth naming plainly. First, there is no latency guarantee.

23
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.839
24 hours is a ceiling, not an estimate, and you can't plan around a typical case

24
00:01:49.839 --> 00:01:53.760
you aren't promised. Second, you don't watch a batch run.

25
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.720
There's no live progress to stream. You submit it,

26
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.760
then poll until it reports that it has ended. Third, there are size limits.

27
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:08.559
100,000 requests, or 256 megabytes per batch, whichever you hit first.

28
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:13.839
One thing that works in your favor, results stay retrievable for 29 days,

29
00:02:13.839 --> 00:02:16.720
so you don't have to be ready the moment a batch finishes.

30
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.520
Every request in a batch comes back in one of four states,

31
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.559
and your handling code needs a branch for each.

32
00:02:24.559 --> 00:02:28.720
Succeeded is the happy path. The message content is right there on the result.

33
00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:32.880
Errored means that request failed, and the distinction matters.

34
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.199
A validation error means the request itself was malformed,

35
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:40.000
so retrying it unchanged just fails again.

36
00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:44.000
A server error is transient and safe to retry as-is.

37
00:02:44.559 --> 00:02:48.240
Cancelled means you cancelled the batch while that request was still queued.

38
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.600
Expired means it didn't finish inside the window, and it just needs resubmitting.

39
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.360
Notice that only one of those four is a finished piece of work.

40
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.399
Treating the rest as failures to investigate is how batch pipelines get noisy.

41
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.279
Now, the correlation problem, and this is the one that bites people.

42
00:03:07.279 --> 00:03:10.800
Batch results come back in whatever order they happen to finish,

43
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.119
which isn't the order you submitted them in,

44
00:03:13.119 --> 00:03:16.960
so you give every request a custom identifier when you submit it,

45
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.399
something that points back at the source document.

46
00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:24.800
When results arrive, you match each one by that identifier.

47
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.839
What you never do is read results positionally,

48
00:03:27.839 --> 00:03:30.639
assuming the first result belongs to the first document.

49
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:36.880
That assumption is wrong, and here's why it's dangerous rather than just incorrect.

50
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.639
It doesn't throw an error.

51
00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:41.039
You get a full set of plausible-looking records

52
00:03:41.039 --> 00:03:44.720
with the data attached to the wrong ones, and nothing anywhere tells you.

53
00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:48.960
Deciding what belongs in a batch comes down to one question.

54
00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.160
Is anything blocked while this runs?

55
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.440
Overnight reporting?

56
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.279
A weekly compliance audit?

57
00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:57.839
Generating tests across a repository?

58
00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:00.240
Backfilling extraction over an archive?

59
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.080
Nobody's waiting, the deadline is measured in hours,

60
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.000
and a 24-hour ceiling fits inside it comfortably.

61
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.360
Now the other side, a check that gates emerge,

62
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.279
anything interactive, anything a downstream pipeline is sitting on.

63
00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:19.920
For those, a 24-hour ceiling isn't a discount you're accepting,

64
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.079
it's an outage you're scheduling.

65
00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.640
The tolerance for delay is the whole decision,

66
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.760
and it's a property of the workflow rather than of the prompt.

67
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.519
There's one constraint that surprises people,

68
00:04:31.519 --> 00:04:35.040
and it's worth stating precisely because it's easy to get backwards.

69
00:04:35.679 --> 00:04:38.799
Batch requests do support tools.

70
00:04:38.799 --> 00:04:42.799
You can define them, and the model can return a tool call in the result.

71
00:04:42.799 --> 00:04:45.440
What you can't do is execute that tool

72
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.399
and feed its result back partway through the request.

73
00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:53.359
A batch request is a single turn submitted once and returned once,

74
00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:56.640
so there's no round trip available to complete the loop in.

75
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.000
That means the agentic pattern, where the model calls a tool,

76
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.640
reads what came back, and decides what to do next,

77
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:03.920
needs the synchronous path.

78
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.160
Batches suit single-turn work, however much of it you have.

79
00:05:09.519 --> 00:05:11.679
Three practical points to close on.

80
00:05:11.679 --> 00:05:15.279
Batching stacks with your other cost controls rather than replacing them.

81
00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:19.279
If every request in the batch shares a large system prompt,

82
00:05:19.279 --> 00:05:21.839
that prompt still caches across them,

83
00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:24.640
so the discount and the cache-saving compound.

84
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.799
And before you commit 10,000 documents,

85
00:05:26.799 --> 00:05:29.839
run the prompt over a small sample and check the output.

86
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.320
A first pass failure at full volume means paying for the whole run,

87
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.640
and then paying again for the resubmission.

88
00:05:37.359 --> 00:05:40.399
Size your submissions against your own deadline, too.

89
00:05:40.399 --> 00:05:42.640
If a result is needed by 9 in the morning,

90
00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.640
submitting at 8 and hoping for the typical case isn't a plan,

91
00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.959
because the ceiling is what you're actually promised.

92
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.760
The expensive mistake with batching isn't the weight.

93
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.279
It's assuming results come back in the order you sent them.

94
00:05:57.279 --> 00:06:00.799
They don't, and a positional read fails quietly,

95
00:06:00.799 --> 00:06:04.959
so wrong answers land on right records and nobody notices for weeks.

96
00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:08.000
Match on your own custom identifier,

97
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.200
and that whole class of bug disappears for you.

98
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.519
Then batching becomes half-priced tokens for work nobody is waiting on,

99
00:06:15.519 --> 00:06:16.640
which is the whole point.

100
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:18.880
That wraps up this lesson.

101
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.000
Thanks for watching.

